[{:db/id "q-342562/field-1/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "77556d4a-1589-5b09-bec1-8ce736a069a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342562/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "83e16358-519e-5455-b910-148e3f8eb26b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342562/field-1/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer-field/choices ["q-342562/field-1/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342562" :question/id #uuid "422cbd5e-38e5-544a-b056-53061bf5421a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342562" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/08ce8625dcad742776846497c4e49b5686a893be85ff1bd5426df8f8b9ce882b.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,1)$ and lies on both $y=f(x)$ and $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $A\\,(1,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(1)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342562/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342562"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-18601/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "fd9afa23-7f21-5bfd-99fe-558a3d95a546" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-18601/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "37916253-c11b-5258-9880-f77aade288fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-18601/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "995de621-ea4f-5be3-9bfa-c3bea3b38f81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-18601/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "73bf5e7b-fa30-5a4d-a081-82a6905392ea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-18601/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "6edc8b38-4fb2-5970-b364-9f9c67e2e9dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-18601/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "4c9b5459-d3ea-5931-864a-91d802f0272f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-18601/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-18601/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-18601/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-18601/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-18601/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-18601/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-18601" :question/id #uuid "2e2a8e81-0143-5ac3-9218-1837a0d8d00a" :question/math-academy-id "q-18601" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/7e881b5cb3613ff72264739b1cbe1214c1041a78d201cfc323476910e7ce8e3c.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(4,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(2,4)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $(2,4)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=4.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [1,5]$ is $1$, hence, ${f}^{′}(4)=1.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-18601/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-18601"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342570/field-1/0f49a4dbd5c742935484b6812e6555790b20d2c8dcba12576f1906584b309432" :answer/id #uuid "95051f6c-2cda-512b-a166-85bf75a4fb6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\ln(4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342570/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f8d23cac-45b4-5c6c-8c7d-2aa0b595e105" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342570/field-1/0f49a4dbd5c742935484b6812e6555790b20d2c8dcba12576f1906584b309432" :answer-field/choices ["q-342570/field-1/0f49a4dbd5c742935484b6812e6555790b20d2c8dcba12576f1906584b309432"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342570" :question/id #uuid "3d0c5039-62c4-5869-afca-b3d561b286f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-342570" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/feb02396a635c3f416b07383b03269e12fde738da3345aa6d6d9fe5350745746.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(1,0.5)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(0.5,1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4},$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(0.5,1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0.5)=1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0.5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0.5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342570/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342570"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-312578/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "1fe6726f-14a9-5251-a055-84ac34260be2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-312578/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "0f65b70c-4513-5d7f-88de-bf7e421f1942" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-312578/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "40350093-241a-55dd-ac9f-9a83f78e707e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-312578/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-312578/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-312578/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-312578" :question/id #uuid "77c43205-3a68-5865-9baf-3488c8c39bcb" :question/math-academy-id "q-312578" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/e00b5083b25408240fb0eef26c898e237437bf9fe0f7fce11e397b9d7683a1c2.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a piecewise linear function $y=f(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(2,0)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $(0,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x),$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)?$\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(0)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $(0,2)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(0)=2.$ \nThe slope of the tangent to $f(x)$ for $x\\in [0,4]$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$, hence, ${f}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is \n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(0) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(0))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-312578/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-312578"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15709/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "62c45b8f-4bd1-5b10-9494-818e9cc1998a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15709/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer/id #uuid "acd009f3-ae84-5bf6-9c44-e7fcdf8b3a66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15709/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "bcc94adc-ca4b-504d-8858-062876eb3fef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15709/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "069c5138-e7b7-5507-822e-4670dae7e2db" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15709/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "13cb97fe-9b74-58cd-89b8-6a209d64a358" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15709/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "48dfbb50-946c-5981-abd7-1282378dd376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15709/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" :answer-field/choices ["q-15709/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-15709/selection/4e2cb386499e1926f074a7bcd422a63175a237204a3823f0fa6c86b56d301873" "q-15709/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-15709/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-15709/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15709" :question/id #uuid "ba779119-012f-5aba-bccf-539b483e38de" :question/math-academy-id "q-15709" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14037063/assets/0d7c1b55cbc73a769f7ab0c0c8e634d54356eb36e77dc7ca0d06ff77d9367162.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of a function $y=f(x)$ and its inverse $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ The point $A$ has coordinates $(-1,2)$ and lies on $y=f(x).$ The point $B$ has coordinates $(2,-1)$ and lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$ If ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2,$ what is $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $B\\,(2,-1)$ lies on ${f}^{-1},$ we have ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1.$\nWe're also told that ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of the inverse function is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-15709/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4270c31c-80f1-5b5d-bca8-e16a47206c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15709"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-312580/field-1/1355f1aec40f88517af1e83a24f03c56ffa2f231ffad50a4d52f975fdcbf6b66" :answer/id #uuid "3c252298-e973-55f9-b09e-7bb41ab18e55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\ln(2)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-312580/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "99ad25f2-831a-51b2-bf5b-1d70cd523db3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-312580/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "50ba0f3b-3db3-5e26-bb8f-963cf1a8bd6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-312580/field-1/1355f1aec40f88517af1e83a24f03c56ffa2f231ffad50a4d52f975fdcbf6b66" :answer-field/choices ["q-312580/field-1/1355f1aec40f88517af1e83a24f03c56ffa2f231ffad50a4d52f975fdcbf6b66" "q-312580/field-1/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-312580" :question/id #uuid "9315fbdb-8a10-5769-ba1d-a74c3c6c47b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-312580" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+{2}^{x}$ and the point $P(0,1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ the slope of $y={f}^{-1}(x)$ at the point $(1,0)$ is$\\,${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(0,1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+{2}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x+{2}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(0,1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,0)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-312580/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-312580"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342583/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "205c7a5e-3ebd-5d2e-8080-e01818835b75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342583/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4aee9e45-6561-505c-b53a-1631b71fe964" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342583/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-342583/field-1/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342583" :question/id #uuid "693dd3d0-3454-5894-a36e-0df2728d3681" :question/math-academy-id "q-342583" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ and the point $P(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & ={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & ={sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342583/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342583"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342579/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "9ac894c4-80f6-5d55-8c9a-7c86cba29ce3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186279595 :answer-field/choices ["q-342579/field-1/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342582/field-1/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555" :answer/id #uuid "635ae1bd-f5f8-56de-b9e5-7ff6a429bfb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342582/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "16ce9f09-7db6-5f63-94a1-ce00fb0d1697" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-342582/field-1/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555" :answer-field/choices ["q-342582/field-1/3a3e10526f40c673b7703a3c103fe7589223997acb2a083d0e3a20b742c55555"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342582" :question/id #uuid "c8afa493-ae6a-5bc8-a39b-9bb74c7ad881" :question/math-academy-id "q-342582" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={e}^{2x}$ and the point $P(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4} \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =2\\cdot 4 \\\\ {f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) & =8.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2,4)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(4) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(4))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342582/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342582"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-75831/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "93b248f2-0ad6-566c-9f25-1c175397f527" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75831/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "b461f741-12b5-584c-8580-dc84fca2ab6d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75831/selection/71b1db0b2a675655ec27a2d2ceef2e86712a68eb78548232215378e0a354fb4c" :answer/id #uuid "000d7775-0402-5005-bbb1-a7a52ebb2cb0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75831/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" :answer/id #uuid "3f1a8b23-aa69-5471-bb76-b991d477d1e8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75831/selection/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d" :answer/id #uuid "a2b4e94b-b9a9-5a3e-8b0f-bcfffccaf066" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75831/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f574523f-5ae6-5179-87fe-870299a88403" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-75831/selection/71b1db0b2a675655ec27a2d2ceef2e86712a68eb78548232215378e0a354fb4c" :answer-field/choices ["q-75831/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-75831/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-75831/selection/71b1db0b2a675655ec27a2d2ceef2e86712a68eb78548232215378e0a354fb4c" "q-75831/selection/c7e2c99672184a6ffc5fe21255ac37862a4c27e4b225c7b645ba47d8f3c76eda" "q-75831/selection/5ab3573096b30d3c5d3c378e667364cbb062f921e9b1002441e594f0774a669d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-75831" :question/id #uuid "38a623ea-a52e-5dd0-8781-88f72b4e9cf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-75831" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(5).$\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $5$ | $-6$ |\n| $3$ | $-3$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(5)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(5)=1$ and ${f}^{′}(1)=-6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(5) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(5))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-6)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-75831/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-75831"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-312583/field-1/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer/id #uuid "c0464f1b-b508-5d46-aab4-a160247b57b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-312583/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd9d412-937f-5c3d-b8be-8ecf613b4921" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-312583/field-1/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer-field/choices ["q-312583/field-1/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-312583" :question/id #uuid "d1490c31-6761-5f3f-8b46-c37dbd062af7" :question/math-academy-id "q-312583" :question/problem "Find $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)$ given the following table of values of functions $f(x)$ and ${f}^{′}(x)$ at some points.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n| $3$ | $2$ | $-5$ |\n\n$({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}.\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=3$ and ${f}^{′}(3)=-5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1})^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-5)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-312583/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-312583"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342589/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "23122002-68de-55a3-8698-55d189a802c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186283013 :answer-field/choices ["q-342589/field-1/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186261590 :question/answer-fields 17592186274991] {:db/id "q-75829/selection/ma-v1/6628defb4eae99b60781c2aa15cc0bf200f23848d6d435b1d0093d1b2f7a4fce/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "7b652385-36d1-5d58-ac68-b26d0a5efd5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75829/selection/ma-v1/6628defb4eae99b60781c2aa15cc0bf200f23848d6d435b1d0093d1b2f7a4fce/99f48c1b72e8eeab33eba2584ff0661456642ba13c82a72d98a76e8e1a07563e" :answer/id #uuid "41dd22ae-0f48-5cae-b59e-79055a39b45d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-75829/selection/ma-v1/6628defb4eae99b60781c2aa15cc0bf200f23848d6d435b1d0093d1b2f7a4fce/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #